Hey there, I have hit a snag:

I have two surveys and the question is whether the results are different
or not. There are eight age cohorts and the variables are categorical
(is news pronounced nooze or nyooze and so on).

I ran t-tests based on the means for each age group, so I have 8 data
points (the means) for Survey 1, and 8 data points for Survey 2. I ran
these tests for all my variables (in a couple of cases there was no
normal distribution, so I used the Wilcoxon test instead).

The results looked fine. But there may be a problem.

The following is evident: the 8 age cohorts do not have the same n's:
they vary from 60 to 120 to 40 and the oldest age cohort has only 15.
I've been trying to run the t-test on the raw data and not the means. I
took the responses of one survey and paired them with a sample, matched
by age group, from the other survey (which has bigger n's). My data
frames look like this:

FQ and PQ is the survey type, 1 & 2... the age cohort (10-year olds,
20-year old), and then the form (categorical).

1       FQ   1  nooze
2       FQ   1 nyooze
3       FQ   1 nyooze
4       FQ   2  nooze
5       FQ  2 nyooze
6       FQ  2 nyooze

168     PQ   1  nooze
169     PQ   1 nyooze
170     PQ   1 nyooze
171     PQ   2 nyooze
172     PQ   2 nyooze
173     PQ   2 nyooze

I ran the procedures in the Gries book (Shapiro Wilk, then t.test)

R gives an error message:

 > t.test(Survey~News, paired=T, data=News)
Error in complete.cases(x, y) : not all arguments have the same length

Any thoughts? I'd appreciate your help.

Stef

